    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, it's clear and professional. I like the tone.\n\nI just ran that through our internal readability checker to see where we stand. The score is sitting around 0.72 (meaning 72% of the words have fewer than three syllables). It's okay, but for this specific demographic, our goal is to get that score closer to 0.85 or 0.90 to ensure there's absolutely no friction for patients who might have cognitive decline or vision issues.\n\nCan you try expanding this into a more detailed section? Specifically, explain what \"systolic\" and \"diastolic\" pressure actually mean and how they relate to the heart. Again, I really don't want you to sacrifice the medical detail just to get a higher simplicity score—I need the definitions to be accurate—but try to keep the language as punchy and simple as possible so the metric moves in the right direction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your systolic number is the peak pressure in your arteries when your heart squeezes hard to pump blood, often called the \"top\" reading. Your diastolic number measures the pressure when your heart rests between beats, known as the \"bottom\" reading. Together, these two numbers tell us exactly how hard your body is working every moment, helping doctors fine-tune your treatment plan. Keeping both numbers in a healthy range reduces strain on your heart and keeps your blood flowing smoothly to all your organs. This simple split is the key to understanding your health report and taking confident steps toward wellness.",
      "source": "target",
